// Solution to 5.3: the two operators meet.
//
// Depth first, then breadth. `graceful_deep` is the refined brick from 5.2; it goes into
// the assembly in place of the plain one, and `fair` and `performant` join it unchanged.
//
// Then count the evidence in the result and compare it with 5.1. `performant` still says,
// in its own file, that the test dataset is trustworthy, as a bare leaf. But that label
// now also belongs to the sub-conclusion the refinement created, so the two meet as one
// node, and performant's leaf arrives already argued.
//
// Nobody edited performant.jd. It got the deeper argument because it worded its evidence
// the same way graceful did.

load "../bricks/fair.jd"
load "../bricks/performant.jd"
load "../bricks/graceful.jd"
load "../bricks/trustworthy.jd"

justification graceful_deep is refine(graceful, trustworthy) {
    hook: "test"
}

// The bricks can be listed in any order: put `performant` first and you get the same
// model. Where two of them meet on a label and disagree about what kind of node it is,
// the merge keeps the kind that can carry an argument, so the refinement's sub-conclusion
// and performant's bare leaf become one sub-conclusion either way round.
justification deployable is assemble(graceful_deep, fair, performant) {
    conclusionLabel: "The classifier is ready for deployment"
    strategyLabel:   "Every quality bar is cleared"
}
